<article class="env-variant" id="05QA">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05QA">Variant <span data-tag="05QA">7.1.2.11</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable cardinal and let $Y$ be a Kan complex which is essentially $\kappa $-small. Suppose we are given a morphism of simplicial sets $e: K \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }}( \Delta ^0, Y)$. If the simplicial set $K$ is essentially $\kappa $-small, then the following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $e$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $e$ exhibits $Y$ as a copower of $\Delta ^0$ by $K$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Beware that, if $K$ is not assumed to be essentially $\kappa $-small, then it is possible for condition $(2)$ to be satisfied while condition $(1)$ is not (Exercise <a data-tag="05QB" href="/tag/05QB">7.1.2.12</a>). </p>
</article>
